<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ammianus Marcellinus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 330–c. 400</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ammianus Marcellinus was a Greek-born Roman soldier and historian. His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> history, of which the books covering 353 to 378 survive, is the main source for the reigns of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantius-ii">Constantius II</a>, Julian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valens">Valens</a>. It describes peoples on and beyond the empire's frontiers, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/picts">Picts</a>, Alans, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huns">Huns</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a>, Chionites and the Austuriani raiders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, and ends with the Roman defeat at Adrianople.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ammianus-marcellinus.jpg" alt="Ammianus Marcellinus" width="320" height="453" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Author of the original work of art is long dead, while the uploader of the picture portraying it released it under a CC BY 3.0 license. · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ammianus_Marcellinus.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ammianus was born around 330 in the Roman Levant, possibly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenicia">Phoenicia</a>, into a noble family of Greek origin. He called himself a former soldier and a Greek, and Greek was his native language, though he also knew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>. His enrolment among the elite protectores domestici, the household guards, indicates a middle-class or higher birth. He entered the army young, under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantius-ii">Constantius II</a>, and was sent to serve under Ursicinus, governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nisibis">Nisibis</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> and magister militum, with whom he campaigned twice in the East.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Ammianus travelled with Ursicinus to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> against Silvanus, an officer who had proclaimed himself emperor in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>. Ursicinus had Silvanus assassinated and stayed to help install Julian as Caesar of Gaul, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>. In 359 Constantius sent Ursicinus back east to meet a Persian invasion led by King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shapur-ii">Shapur II</a>. Ammianus served again as a staff officer and was at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amida">Amida</a> when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> besieged and sacked it; he barely escaped with his life.</p><p>After Constantius dismissed Ursicinus, Ammianus seems to have retired, though some modern scholars think he continued to serve. He accompanied Julian, whom he admired, against the Alamanni and the Sassanids, and went with the new emperor Jovian as far as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a>. He was living at Antioch in 372, when he claims to have witnessed how Theodorus and others, suspected of divining <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valens">Valens</a>'s successor, were tortured and punished. He later settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. The year of his death is unknown, but scholars place it between 392 and 400 at the latest.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in the 380s Ammianus wrote the Res gestae in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>, a history from the accession of Nerva in 96 to the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valens">Valens</a> at Adrianople in 378, in effect a continuation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacitus">Tacitus</a>. It praises the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serapeum-of-alexandria">Serapeum of Alexandria</a> as the glory of the empire, so it was presumably finished before the building's destruction in 391. Of its thirty-one books the first thirteen are lost. The eighteen that survive, covering 353 to 378, are the foundation of modern understanding of the fourth-century empire. Its report of the earthquake and tsunami of 21 July 365 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> accurately describes the sequence of quake, retreating sea and sudden wave.</p><p>Scholars generally describe him as a pagan tolerant of Christians. He remarked that no wild beasts are so hostile to men as Christian sects are to one another, and he condemned Julian for his attachment to sacrifice and for his edict barring Christians from teaching posts. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-gibbon">Edward Gibbon</a> judged him an accurate and faithful guide, while Kimberly Kagan argues that his battle accounts stress the soldiers' experience at the cost of the larger picture. Almost every <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> derives from a ninth-century text, Vatican lat. 1873, made at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fulda">Fulda</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ammianus Marcellinus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Ammianus Marcellinus</b> wrote that the Alans worshipped a naked sword thrust into the ground as their god of war, and foretold the future with willow rods.</blockquote><a href="/empire/alans">Alans · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Julian restored it, and in 357 at the Battle of Strasbourg his army of about 13,000 defeated a coalition under Chnodomar and his nephew Serapio, which <b>Ammianus Marcellinus</b> put at some 35,000 men.</blockquote><a href="/empire/alemanni">Alemanni · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Under the Sasanian Empire Albanian cavalry served in Persian campaigns, and <b>Ammianus Marcellinus</b> records Albanians among the forces of Shapur II at the siege of Amida in 359.</blockquote><a href="/empire/caucasian-albania">Caucasian Albania · Military</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Ammianus Marcellinus</b> says most of their diet was meat; they drank mare's milk and probably made cheese and kumis.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hunnic-empire">Hunnic Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Ammianus Marcellinus</b> says that in the 360s they were divided into two peoples, the Dicalydones and the Verturiones, the latter probably the ancestors of the later kingdom of Fortriu.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-picts">Kingdom of the Picts · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 367 a combined attack, which <b>Ammianus Marcellinus</b> called a barbarian conspiracy, killed the Count of the Saxon Shore and captured the Duke, and order was restored only by Count Theodosius in 368–369.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-britain">Roman Britain · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=36.2,36.15&amp;year=375" aria-label="Antioch on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5460 857 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Ammianus Marcellinus"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5910 1127h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5910 1127h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=36.2,36.15&amp;year=375">What was here in 375 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
